{
136: {skill_tested: "Identify an explicitly stated benefit of linked storage sites.", question: "According to the second paragraph, why are orders from several farms combined at a hub?", option1: "To eliminate labels before delivery", option2: "To create loads large enough for economical transport", option3: "To require every farm to grow the same crop", option4: "To keep all produce at one temperature", correctAnswer: "To create loads large enough for economical transport", explanation: "Step 1: Farms bring produce to nearby cooling sites.<br><br>Step 2: The hub combines smaller orders into loads that can be transported economically.<br><br>Answer: To create loads large enough for economical transport", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
137: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of an abstract term.", question: "As used in the eighth paragraph, the word 'feasible' most nearly means:", option1: "identical for every member.", option2: "required by cooperative rules.", option3: "practical or possible.", option4: "measured only by profit.", correctAnswer: "practical or possible.", explanation: "Step 1: Reliable cooling can make delicate crops less risky.<br><br>Step 2: It expands what farms can reasonably choose to grow, so 'feasible' means practical or possible.<br><br>Answer: practical or possible.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
138: {skill_tested: "Identify a central idea about cooperative networks.", question: "Which statement best expresses the passage's central idea?", option1: "A cooperative works best when all members follow identical harvest schedules.", option2: "The largest storage hub should control which crops every member produces.", option3: "Route efficiency alone provides a complete measure of cooperative success.", option4: "Shared infrastructure can turn varied needs into collective capacity, but it requires resilient systems and fair coordination.", correctAnswer: "Shared infrastructure can turn varied needs into collective capacity, but it requires resilient systems and fair coordination.", explanation: "Step 1: Different harvest peaks help members share limited storage and transport.<br><br>Step 2: Hub failures, unequal access, reservations, information, and fair rules determine whether that sharing remains useful.<br><br>Answer: Shared infrastructure can turn varied needs into collective capacity, but it requires resilient systems and fair coordination.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
139: {skill_tested: "Explain an abstract description of pooled capacity.", question: "The statement that empty space can move in an accounting sense primarily means that:", option1: "unused capacity can be reassigned among members whose peak needs occur at different times.", option2: "warehouse walls are physically shifted between harvests.", option3: "empty crates are transported to the largest farm.", option4: "members may reserve unlimited space without updating forecasts.", correctAnswer: "unused capacity can be reassigned among members whose peak needs occur at different times.", explanation: "Step 1: Farms reach peak harvest at different times.<br><br>Step 2: Coordinators compare reservations with actual use and release unused capacity.<br><br>Step 3: The physical room remains fixed, but the right to use portions of it shifts among members.<br><br>Answer: unused capacity can be reassigned among members whose peak needs occur at different times.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
140: {skill_tested: "Analyze why information functions as part of physical logistics.", question: "Why does the passage describe a mislabeled crate as occupying a false position in the schedule?", option1: "Its label physically prevents other crates from fitting in the room.", option2: "Its incorrect information can cause storage and transport decisions to be made for the wrong destination or time.", option3: "It changes the temperature requirement of every nearby product.", option4: "It proves that the cooperative no longer needs actual storage space.", correctAnswer: "Its incorrect information can cause storage and transport decisions to be made for the wrong destination or time.", explanation: "Step 1: Coordinators reassign loads and preserve orders through accurate, prompt updates.<br><br>Step 2: A wrong destination makes planners allocate route and timing capacity inaccurately.<br><br>Step 3: The crate therefore disrupts the informational arrangement even while occupying an ordinary physical location.<br><br>Answer: Its incorrect information can cause storage and transport decisions to be made for the wrong destination or time.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
141: {skill_tested: "Infer why hub efficiency can reduce resilience.", question: "The central hub can become a vulnerability primarily because:", option1: "smaller sites are prohibited from cooling produce.", option2: "all hub equipment must be operated without temperature alarms.", option3: "concentrating many farms' goods and transfers makes one failure affect a large portion of the network.", option4: "produce cannot be moved after reaching the hub.", correctAnswer: "concentrating many farms' goods and transfers makes one failure affect a large portion of the network.", explanation: "Step 1: The hub combines orders and coordinates major storage and transport functions.<br><br>Step 2: A power failure there can affect produce belonging to many farms simultaneously.<br><br>Step 3: The same concentration that creates efficiency increases the reach of a single disruption.<br><br>Answer: concentrating many farms' goods and transfers makes one failure affect a large portion of the network.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
142: {skill_tested: "Analyze how layered capacity balances competing access risks.", question: "The base, flexible, and emergency capacity layers primarily help the cooperative avoid:", option1: "tracking actual storage use after reservations are made.", option2: "serving farms with different harvest periods.", option3: "maintaining any unused room for unexpected events.", option4: "both inflexible advance forecasts and unrestricted last-minute competition.", correctAnswer: "both inflexible advance forecasts and unrestricted last-minute competition.", explanation: "Step 1: Base reservations provide dependable access before harvest.<br><br>Step 2: Flexible and emergency pools accommodate uncertainty closer to actual need.<br><br>Step 3: Combining layers prevents either strict forecasts or immediate competition from controlling all capacity.<br><br>Answer: both inflexible advance forecasts and unrestricted last-minute competition.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
143: {skill_tested: "Synthesize efficiency and equity in evaluating a network.", question: "Why might a lightly used rural cooling site still be valuable to the cooperative?", option1: "It may provide distant farms their only practical access, a benefit not captured by utilization alone.", option2: "It necessarily stores more crates than the central hub during peak weeks.", option3: "Its low use guarantees that it cannot experience a power failure.", option4: "It allows members to avoid all reservation rules.", correctAnswer: "It may provide distant farms their only practical access, a benefit not captured by utilization alone.", explanation: "Step 1: Distance and fuel costs make access unequal, and a route can be efficient while serving only large farms.<br><br>Step 2: A rural site may have fewer crates but connect otherwise excluded members.<br><br>Step 3: Its network value therefore includes geographic access, not merely the percentage of storage filled.<br><br>Answer: It may provide distant farms their only practical access, a benefit not captured by utilization alone.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
144: {skill_tested: "Derive how difference can strengthen a coordinated network.", question: "The phrase 'coordinated difference' in the final paragraph most strongly emphasizes that:", option1: "members must surrender all independent production decisions to the hub.", option2: "varied crops and schedules can smooth shared demand when linked by accurate information and fair rules.", option3: "every uncertainty should be transferred from one farm to all others.", option4: "the network succeeds only when every crate passes through one building.", correctAnswer: "varied crops and schedules can smooth shared demand when linked by accurate information and fair rules.", explanation: "Step 1: Farms require storage at different times, allowing unused capacity to be reassigned.<br><br>Step 2: Those differences become useful only through reservations, updates, transport, and equitable allocation.<br><br>Step 3: Coordination turns variation into complementary demand without erasing each farm's choices.<br><br>Answer: varied crops and schedules can smooth shared demand when linked by accurate information and fair rules.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
